ts are already covered by the horizontal lines.\n     Thus we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); the\n   trivial bounds show that no other \\(k\\) can occur. Hence the required set of\n   admissible \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the set  \n\n\\[\nU_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid b\\ge n-k+1\\}\n\\]\n\nis in bijection with  \n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(a',b')\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a'+b'\\le k+1\\}\n\\]\n\nvia the map \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a',b')=(a,\\;b-(n-k))\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in U_k\\) then \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); putting\n\\(b'=b-(n-k)\\) gives \\(b'\\ge 1\\) and  \n\n\\[\na'+b'=a+b-(n-k)\\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nConversely, given \\((a',b')\\in T_k\\) define \\(a=a'\\) and\n\\(b=b'+(n-k)\\); then \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nFor every integer \\(k\\ge 1\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that\ncover all points of \\(T_k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe points  \n\n\\[\nP_j=(j,\\;k+1-j)\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,k)\n\\]\n\nbelong to \\(T_k\\) because \\(j+(k+1-j)=k+1\\).  \nChoose pairwise distinct real numbers \\(m_1,\\dots ,m_k\\) with  \n\n\\[\nm_j\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\qquad\\text{for all }j .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(j\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y-(k+1-j)=m_j\\bigl(x-j\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_j\\) passes through \\(P_j\\), has slope \\(m_j\\) (hence is sunny),\nand the slopes are distinct, so the 