--------\n### 1.  Upper bound\n\nSince we must use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\)\n\nAssume that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).\nLet the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines be non‑sunny; each of them is either horizontal,\nvertical or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nConsider the rows (constant \\(b\\)) of the triangle \\(S_n\\) from bottom to top.\nBecause there are only \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines, at most \\(n-2\\) rows can be\ncompletely covered by a horizontal line.  Hence there exist two consecutive rows,\nsay rows \\(t\\) and \\(t-1\\) with \\(t\\ge 3\\), that are **not** completely covered.\nIn each of these rows at least one point of \\(S_n\\) remains uncovered.\n\nChoose the left‑most uncovered point of row \\(t\\); it must be \\((1,t)\\) because\nany uncovered point in a higher column would force a point \\((1,t)\\) to be\nuncovered as well.  Denote  \n\n\\[\nA=(1,t).\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, let \\(B\\) be the left‑most uncovered point of row \\(t-1\\); then  \n\n\\[\nB=(1,t-1).\n\\]\n\nSince column \\(1\\) already contains the two points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the third\nuncovered point in row \\(t-1\\) cannot lie in column \\(1\\); the left‑most possibility\nis column \\(2\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\nC=(2,t-1).\n\\]\n\nThus the three uncovered points are exactly the triple  \n\n\\[\nA=(1,t),\\qquad B=(1,t-1),\\qquad C=(2,t-1) \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwith \\(t\\ge 3\\).\n\nThe slopes of the three lines determined by pairs of these points are\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{slope}(AB)=\\infty,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{slope}(BC)=0,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{slope}(AC)=-1 .\n\\]\n\nAll three slopes are forbidden for a sunny line.  Consequently each sunny line\ncan contain **at most one** of the points \\(A,B,C\\).  With only two sunny lines\nwe can cover at most two of them, leaving at least one point of \\(S_n\\) uncovered.\nThis contradiction shows that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does\nnot exist.\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\neq 2\\quad\\text{for every }n\\ge 3}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n .\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\); all lines are non‑sunny.\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nUse the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\).  \nThe only point of \\(S_n\\) not yet covered is \\((1,n)\\).  \nThe line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y-n = x-1\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(1\\) (sunny) and passes through \\((1,n)\\).  Thus we have exactly one\nsunny line.\n\nBoth constructions work for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Covering a right‑angled triangular lattice with \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(k\\ge 3\\))\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\ge 3\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n\\(T_k\\) contains \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points and is a right‑angled triangle with\nvertices \\((1,k),\\;(k,1),\\;(1,1)\\).\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nThere exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines whose union equals \\(T_k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) set  \n\n\\[\nP_j=(j,\\;k+1-j)\\in T_k .\n\\]\n\nChoose distinct real numbers  \n\n\\[\nm_j\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+2\\}\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,k),\n\\]\n\nso that each \\(m_j\\) differs from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y-(k+1-j)=m_j\\bigl(x-j\\bigr). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_j\\) is \\(m_j\\); therefore each \\(L_j\\) is sunny, and the\ndifferent slopes guarantee that the lines are distinct.\n\nFor a fixed \\(j\\) the points of \\(T_k\\) that lie on \\(L_j\\) are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_j=\n\\{(j,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le k+1-j\\}\\cup\n\\{(a,k+1-j)\\mid 1\\le a\\le j\\}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, substituting \\(x=j\\) in (9) yields \\(y=k+1-j\\), and substituting\n\\(y=k+1-j\\) yields \\(x=j\\); all intermediate integer points on the same row or\ncolumn satisfy the equation because the slope is non‑zero and non‑\\(-1\\).\n\nThe sets \\(\\mathcal L_j\\) are pairwise disjoint: a point belonging to\n\\(\\mathcal L_j\\) has either its first coordinate equal to \\(j\\) or its second\ncoordinate equal to \\(k+1-j\\); no other \\(\\mathcal L_{j'}\\) with \\(j'\\neq j\\) can\ncontain it.  Moreover  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{j=1}^{k}\\mathcal L_j = T_k,\n\\]\n\nbecause every point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) satisfies either \\(a\\le b\\) (hence lies in\n\\(\\mathcal L_a\\)) or \\(a>b\\) (hence lies in \\(\\mathcal L_{b}\\)).  Consequently the\n\\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) cover all points of \\(T_k\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Construction for general \\(n\\) and \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 4\\) and let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Horizontal part.**  \n   Place the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_i:\\; y=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   They cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate is \\(\\le n-k\\).\n\n2. **Uncovered part.**  \n   The points that remain uncovered are  \n\n   \\[\n   U_k=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid b\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Translating them downward by the vector \\((0,n-k)\\) gives a bijection  \n\n   \\[\n   \\phi_k:(a,b)\\mapsto (a,\\;b-(n-k))\n   \\]\n\n   from \\(U_k\\) onto the triangle \\(T_k\\) defined in (8).\n\n3. **Sunny part.**  \n   Apply Lemma 2 to obtain \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) covering \\(T_k\\).\n   Translate each line back by the vector \\((0,n-k)\\); the slopes are unchanged,\n   so the translated lines remain sunny and distinct.  They cover exactly the set\n   \\(U_k\\).\n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-k}\\}\\cup\\{\\text