xis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}}\\qquad\\text{for every integer }n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nIn particular, \\(k=2\\) and every \\(k\\ge 4\\) are impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1.  Write the required set of lattice points as  \n\n    \\[\n    S_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n    \\]\n\n2.  Show that \\(k=0\\) (all horizontal lines) and \\(k=1\\) (all horizontal lines except one sunny line through the unique uncovered point) are always realizable.\n\n3.  Prove that \\(k=2\\) is never possible: after using \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines (e.g. the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\)) the three points  \n\n    \\[\n    (1,n),\\;(1,n-1),\\;(2,n-1)\n    \\]\n\n    remain uncovered, and any line through two of them is non‑sunny; consequently two sunny lines cannot cover all three.\n\n4.  Reduce the problem for \\(k\\ge 3\\) to the case \\(k=n\\): if we can cover the triangle \\(S_{k}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, then for any \\(n\\ge k\\) we cover the lower part of \\(S_{n}\\) with the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\) and the upper part with the \\(k\\) sunny lines (translated upward). Hence it suffices to decide for which \\(k\\) the set \\(S_{k}\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5.  Show that \\(k=3\\) is possible by the explicit three sunny lines  \n\n    \\[\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    L_{1}&:\\;y=x      &&\\text{(through }(1,1),(2,2)),\\\\\n    L_{2}&:\\;y-3=-2(x-1) &&\\text{(through }(1,3),(2,1)),\\\\\n    L_{3}&:\\;y-2=\\tfrac12(x-1) &&\\text{(through }(1,2),(3,1)).\n    \\end{aligned}\n    \\]\n\n    These three lines cover all six points of \\(S_{3}\\).\n\n6.  Prove that for every \\(k\\ge 4\\) a covering of \\(S_{k}\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines is impossible.  \n    *   Each side of the right‑angled triangle \\(S_{k}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) lattice points:\n        \\[\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        C&=\\{(1,t)\\mid 1\\le t\\le k\\},\\\\\n        R&=\\{(s,1)\\mid 1\\le s\\le k\\},\\\\\n        H&=\\{(i,k+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}.\n        \\end{aligned}\n        \\]\n    *   A sunny line cannot be parallel to any side, therefore it meets each side in **at most one** point.\n    *   With \\(k\\) sunny lines we can meet each side in at most \\(k\\) points, so every line must meet **exactly one** point of each side.\n    *   Consequently each line would contain three points, one from each side.  \n        However three points taken from the three distinct sides of a right‑angled triangle are never collinear unless two of them are vertices of the triangle.  \n        The only possible collinear triples are  \n        \\((1,k),(k,1),(1,1)\\); any line containing two of these vertices has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), i.e. it is non‑sunny – a contradiction.\n    *   Hence no family of \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover all points of \\(S_{k}\\) when \\(k\\ge 4\\).\n\n7.  Combining the above, the only values of \\(k\\) for which a configuration exists are \\(k=0,1,3\\).  \n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\n  \\]\n\n  are distinct, non‑sunny and each point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on exactly one of them.\n\n* \\(k=1\\): use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1\n  \\]\n\n  which cover all points of \\(S_{n}\\) except \\((1,n)\\).  \n  The line  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y-n = x-1\\qquad(\\text{slope }1)\n  \\]\n\n  passes through \\((1,n)\\) and is sunny.  Thus a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \nTake the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines to be the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2 .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_{n}\\) with second coordinate \\(\\le n-2\\) are already covered.  \nThe three points  \n\n\\[\nA=(1,n),\\qquad B=(1,n-1),\\qquad C=(2,n-1)\n\\]\n\nremain uncovered.  \n\nAny line through two of \\(A,B,C\\) has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); indeed \\(\\overline{AC}\\) has slope \\(-1\\)).  \nHence a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points.  \nWith only two sunny lines at least one of \\(A,B,C\\) stays uncovered – a contradiction.  \nTherefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Reduction to the triangle \\(S_{k}\\)\n\nSuppose we have a configuration for some \\(n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(k\\ge3\\)).  \nRemove the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\).  \nThe points that are still uncovered are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU_{k}= \\{(a,b)\\in S_{n}\\mid b\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf we translate the coordinates by the vector \\((0,n-k)\\) (i.e. replace \\((a,b)\\) by \\((a,b-(n-k))\\)), the set \\(U_{k}\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\nS_{k}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) exists **iff** the triangle \\(S_{k}\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nConsequently we only have to decide for which integers \\(k\\ge3\\) the set \\(S_{k}\\) admits such a covering.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  A covering for \\(k=3\\)\n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:\\;y=x,\\\\\nL_{2}&:\\;y-3=-2(x-1),\\\\\nL_{3}&:\\;y-2=\\tfrac12(x-1)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(1,-2,\\tfrac12\\), none of which is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nTheir integer points are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:\\;(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\nL_{2}&:\\;(1,3),(2,1),\\\\\nL_{3}&:\\;(1,2),(3,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThese six points are exactly the elements of \\(S_{3}\\).  \nHence \\(k=3\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge4\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\ge4\\) and assume that \\(k\\) sunny lines cover all points of \\(S_{k}\\).  \nDenote the three sides of the right‑angled triangle \\(S_{k}\\) by  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC&=\\{(1,t)\\mid 1\\le t\\le k\\}\\quad\\text{(the left side)},\\\\\nR&=\\{(s,1)\\mid 1\\le s\\le k\\}\\quad\\text{(the bottom side)},\\\\\nH&=\\{(i,k+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\quad\\text{(the hypotenuse)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach side contains exactly \\(k\\) lattice points.\n\n*Because a sunny line is not parallel to any of the three sides, it can meet each side in at most one point.*  \nHence a sunny line can contain **at most three** points of \\(S_{k}\\), one from each side.\n\nSince there are \\(k\\) points on each side and only \\(k\\) lines, every line must meet **exactly one** point of each side; otherwise some side would have fewer than \\(k\\) intersection points, contradicting the fact that all its points must be covered.\n\nConsequently each of the \\(k\\) lines contains a triple  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(1,t_i\\bigr),\\qquad\\bigl(s_i,1\\bigr),\\qquad\\bigl(i,k+1-i\\bigr)\n\\]\n\nwith \\(t_i,s_i,i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  \nThese three points are collinear.\n\nHowever three points taken from the three distinct sides of a right‑angled triangle are never collinear, except when two of them are vertices of the triangle.  \nThe only possible collinear triples are\n\n\\[\n(1,k),\\;(k,1),\\;(1,1),\n\\]\n\nbut any line containing two of these vertices has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (the hypotenuse), i.e. it is **non‑sunny**.  \nThus a sunny line cannot contain a collinear triple of the required form, a contradiction.\n\nTherefore no family of \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover all points of \\(S_{k}\\) when \\(k\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\n*   \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are always realizable (Section 1).\n*   \\(k=2\\) is never realizable (Section 2).\n*   \\(k=3\\) is realizable (Section 4).\n*   No \\(k\\ge4\\) is realizable (Section 5).\n\nHence for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
