\(k\\ge 4\\).\n\n7.  Combining the above, the only values of \\(k\\) for which a configuration exists are \\(k=0,1,3\\).  \n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\n  \\]\n\n  are distinct, non‑sunny and each point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on exactly one of them.\n\n* \\(k=1\\): use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1\n  \\]\n\n  which cover all points of \\(S_{n}\\) except \\((1,n)\\).  \n  The line  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y-n = x-1\\qquad(\\text{slope }1)\n  \\]\n\n  passes through \\((1,n)\\) and is sunny.  Thus a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \nTake the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines to be the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2 .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_{n}\\) with second coordinate \\(\\le n-2\\) are already covered.  \nThe three points  \n\n\\[\nA=(1,n),\\qquad B=(1,n-1),\\qquad C=(2,n-1)\n\\]\n\nremain uncovered.  \n\nAny line through two of \\(A,B,C\\) has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); indeed \\(\\overline{AC}\\) has slope \\(-1\\)).  \nHence a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points.  \nWith only two sunny lines at least one of \\(A,B,C\\) stays uncovered – a contradiction.  \nTherefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Reduction to the triangle \\(S_{k}\\)\n\nSuppose we have a conf