((a,b-(n-k))\\)), the set \\(U_{k}\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\nS_{k}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) exists **iff** the triangle \\(S_{k}\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nConsequently we only have to decide for which integers \\(k\\ge3\\) the set \\(S_{k}\\) admits such a covering.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  A covering for \\(k=3\\)\n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:\\;y=x,\\\\\nL_{2}&:\\;y-3=-2(x-1),\\\\\nL_{3}&:\\;y-2=\\tfrac12(x-1)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(1,-2,\\tfrac12\\), none of which is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nTheir integer points are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:\\;(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\nL_{2}&:\\;(1,3),(2,1),\\\\\nL_{3}&:\\;(1,2),(3,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThese six points are exactly the elements of \\(S_{3}\\).  \nHence \\(k=3\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge4\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\ge4\\) and assume that \\(k\\) sunny lines cover all points of \\(S_{k}\\).  \nDenote the three sides of the right‑angled triangle \\(S_{k}\\) by  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC&=\\{(1,t)\\mid 1\\le t\\le k\\}\\quad\\text{(the left side)},\\\\\nR&=\\{(s,1)\\mid 1\\le s\\le k\\}\\quad\\text{(the bottom side)},\\\\\nH&=\\{(i,k+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\quad\\text{(the hypotenuse)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach side contains exactly \\(k\\) lattice points.\n\n*Because a sunny line is not parallel to any of the three sides, it can meet each side in at most one point.*  \nHence a sunny line can contain **at most three** points of \\(S_{k}\\), one from each side.\n\nSince there are \\(k\\) points on each side and only \\(k\\) lines, every line must meet **exactly one** point of each side; otherwise some side would have fewer than \\(k\\) intersection points, contradicting the fact that all its points must be covered.\n\nConsequently each of the \\(k\\) lines contains a triple  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(1,t_i\\bigr),\\qquad\\bigl(s_i,1\\bigr),\\qquad\\bigl(i,k+1-i\\bigr)\n\\]\n\nwith \\(t_i,s_i,i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  \nThese three points are collinear.\n\nHowever three points taken from the three distinct sides of a right‑angled triangle are never collinear, except when two of them are vertices of the triangle.  \nThe only possible collinear triples are\n\n\\[\n(1,k),\\;(k,1),\\;(1,1),\n\\]\n\nbut any line containing two of these vertices has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (the hypotenuse), i.e. it is **non‑sunny**.  \nThus a sunny line cannot contain a collinear triple of the required form, a contradiction.\n\nTherefore no family of \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover all points of \\(S_{k}\\) when \\(k\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\n*   \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are always realizable (Section 1).\n*   \\(k=2\\) is never realizable (Section 2).\n*   \\(k=3\\) is realizable (Section 4).\n*   No \\(k\\ge4\\) is realizable (Section 5).\n\nHence for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
